When [[@voyager|Voyager 2]] flew past in August 1989, it photographed a dark oval storm about as wide as Earth. Scientists called it the Great Dark Spot. It looked a bit like Jupiter's Great Red Spot, which has lasted for centuries.

But when the [[@hubble|Hubble Space Telescope]] looked in 1994, the Great Dark Spot had vanished. Since then Hubble has watched other dark storms appear and fade over a few years. Bright white clouds of frozen methane race around the planet, some of them circling it in about 16 hours.

