Before fridges, the only way to keep things really cold was ice. In winter, workers in the north-eastern USA sawed blocks of ice from frozen lakes and stored them under sawdust. A businessman from Boston named Frederic Tudor began shipping this ice to hot places. In 1833, one of his ships, the *Tuscany*, reached Calcutta, now [[@kolkata|Kolkata]], after about four months at sea. Nearly half the ice had melted on the way, but the rest sold quickly to people desperate for a cold drink.

Ice became a luxury in British India. In Madras, now [[@chennai|Chennai]], a round building on the beach was built in 1842 to store Tudor's ice. Later it became famous as the place where [[@swami-vivekananda|Swami Vivekananda]] stayed in 1897, and today it is called Vivekanandar Illam.

