चेचक से इस तरह लड़ने वाले जेनर पहले नहीं थे। सदियों से भारत, चीन और अफ़्रीका के कुछ हिस्सों में लोग वेरियोलेशन करते थे: चेचक के फफोले से ज़रा-सा पदार्थ लेकर किसी सेहतमंद इंसान की त्वचा या नाक में डाल देना। यह टीके से ज़्यादा जोखिम भरा था, पर अक्सर इससे हल्की बीमारी होती और ज़िंदगी भर का बचाव मिल जाता।

1721 में अमेरिका के बोस्टन में ओनेसिमस नाम के एक अफ़्रीकी गुलाम ने पादरी कॉटन मैदर को बताया कि उनकी अपनी धरती पर यह कैसे किया जाता था। एक प्रकोप के दौरान मैदर ने इस पर ज़ोर दिया, और इसे आज़माने वाले बहुत-से लोग बच गए।

{{story:refrigerator}}

Walking is controlled falling. When you take a step, you tip forward and put a foot out to catch yourself. Robots that walk dynamically do the same.

The key idea is to keep the robot's [[centre-of-mass|centre of mass]], its balance point, over the area between its feet, or to move the feet quickly under it. When someone shoves a robot sideways, it doesn't stiffen. It takes quick steps in the direction it's being pushed, the way you would on a bumpy bus. Engineers at Boston Dynamics famously filmed people kicking their robots to show how well they recovered.

{{story:bicycle-balance}}

Engineers call this kind of train an electric multiple unit. Mumbai's local trains and most [[@metro-trains|metro trains]] work the same way. Spreading the motors out has several advantages. The train grips the rails better, because many wheels are pushing, not just a few at the front. It can reach high speed in a short distance, which saves time on routes with lots of stops. And if one motor fails, the others can usually keep the train going to the next station.

When the train slows down, its motors work in reverse, like generators. They turn some of the train's speed back into electricity and feed it into the overhead wires. That's called regenerative braking, and it saves a good share of the energy.

